# Expectation record for 11_literals_and_strings/002_string_literal_handling.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_string_literal_handling
area               = 11_literals_and_strings
description        = Concatenation, indexing, embedded NUL and termination of string literals
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
No string literal is ever modified, which would be undefined behaviour.  Every element is
read through unsigned char, so no plain-char signedness difference can be observed, and
every index is inside the literal's own size, the terminator included.  The source file is
pure US-ASCII.  No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
